Found 2026-08-31 by the B065 lane while auditing mid-test directory removals.
NOT fixed there: it is outside B065's scope and the location looks deliberate,
so it is filed rather than folded in.

## Defect

`src/typedb/index.rs` (`mod b046_tests`, ~line 3717):

    fn scratch_dir(tag: &str) -> PathBuf {
        let dir = PathBuf::from(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"))
            .join("target")
            .join(format!("test-typedb-b046-{tag}-{}", std::process::id()));
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
        std::fs::create_dir_all(&dir).unwrap();
        dir
    }

Exactly the defect B065 fixed - `remove_dir_all` on the way IN and never on the
way out, with a fresh pid every run - but it builds under the repo's `target/`
rather than `std::env::temp_dir()`.

## Why B065 missed it

B065 found its population by grepping `temp_dir()`. This helper never calls it,
so it was invisible both to that sweep and to the `/tmp` measurement, and the
coordinator scoped B065 to `/tmp` prefixes explicitly.

## Measured 2026-08-31

    36  <b065-b066 worktree>/target/test-typedb-b046-*
    56  /home/xertrov/src/lsp-openplanet/target/test-typedb-b046-*

Small, and `target/` is gitignored and periodically blown away, which is why
nobody has noticed. It is still one accumulating directory per test per run,
and it survives every run that does not `cargo clean`.

## Work

The docstring says the location is deliberate: "Keeping it below the repository
makes these tests independent of a home or `/tmp`." Do NOT move it to `/tmp`
merely to reuse `crate::testing::TempDir` - that reverses a considered
decision. Either:

- give `TempDir` a constructor taking an explicit parent
  (`TempDir::in_dir(parent, label)`), keeping `Drop` and the uniqueness
  suffix, and use it here; or
- decide the under-`target/` placement no longer earns its keep - the whole
  suite now runs under an isolated `TMPDIR` in B065's gate - and convert it to
  a plain `TempDir`.

The first preserves the stated intent and is the smaller change.

## Test plan

Same shape as B065's, and the same warning applies: measure, do not inspect.
Count `<repo>/target/test-typedb-b046-*` before and after two consecutive runs
of `cargo test --lib b046` and assert it does not grow. Include the unwind-path
variant - a panicking test must clean up too - since that is the case a
trailing `remove_dir_all` cannot cover.

## Note for whoever picks this up

`bl` mutating commands refuse to run from a git worktree AND exit 0 while doing
so, printing an ERROR to stdout. The first attempt to file this bug looked like
it succeeded and created nothing. Verify the ID exists after filing.

## Related

B065 (same defect in `/tmp`; `crate::testing::TempDir` is the guard to reuse),
B046 (the tests this helper serves).

## CORRECTION to the filing-trap note (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

The note above says `bl` mutating commands "exit 0 while refusing". **That is
not what happens, and the distinction changes the countermeasure.** Measured
from `.worktrees/b064`:

    bl bug --title "…"                 -> exit 1     (correct)
    bl bug --title "…" 2>&1 | tail -3  -> exit 0     (WRONG, and this is the trap)
    echo body | bl append B060         -> exit 1     (correct)

`bl` refuses correctly, exits non-zero, and prints an unmissable message:

    ERROR: 'bl bug' is a mutating backlog command and cannot be run from a
    git worktree.

**The masking is ours, not `bl`'s.** A shell pipeline reports the exit status of
its LAST command, so `bl … | tail` or `bl … | grep` discards `bl`'s failure and
reports `tail`'s success. The coordinator had been invoking it as
`bl bug --title "…" 2>&1 | tail -3` all batch — the same trap, surviving only
because those calls ran in the main checkout where `bl` succeeded anyway.

Note `bl append` via heredoc is SAFE: `bl` is last in that pipeline, so its
status propagates.

**So do not file a defect against `bl`.** The countermeasures are:

1. Do not pipe a mutating command through `tail`/`grep`. Run it bare and let
   the output show.
2. If you must pipe, check `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` (bash) / `$pipestatus[1]` (zsh),
   not `$?`.
3. Verify the artifact regardless — `ls .backlog/bugs/<ID>-*` — because that is
   what actually caught this. Reading the output and noticing it looked like an
   echo rather than a confirmation was the right instinct even though the
   inferred cause was wrong.

The advice in the original note still stands and is worth repeating: anyone who
filed a ticket from a worktree today should confirm the ID exists. The
coordinator has verified B069-B075 all exist with bodies, and that every
coordinator append landed.